# The approach and the landing, in a light aeroplane: from the point the
# aerodrome is in sight to the aeroplane stopped on the runway.
#
# **The source of the speeds is each aeroplane's own.** `vref` is the speed
# over the threshold - by convention 1.3 times the stall speed with everything
# down, which `sim::approach_speeds` works out from the published stall in
# `assets/figures/<id>.xml`. The four light aircraft do not share one: writing
# a number here would hold a Cessna to a Cub's speed and teach nothing. So
# every speed below is `vref` with an offset.
#
#   name TEXT...                   what the lesson is called
#   teaches CLASS                  which class of aircraft it is for
#   stage TEXT...                  a stage, in the order flown
#   do TEXT...                     what to do at this stage
#   until PROPERTY <=|>= VALUE     what ends the stage
#   hold PROPERTY LOW HIGH TEXT... a band that must hold for the whole stage
#   need PROPERTY <=|>= VALUE TEXT...  what must be true by the stage's end
#
# with `#` beginning a comment; src/sim/lesson.hpp owns the format.
#
# **The bands are set from what a correctly flown approach does**, measured by
# flying all four light aeroplanes down a three-degree glidepath with the
# approach autopilot and watching the speed, not guessed. An approach is flown
# a little fast early and settles towards `vref` over the threshold, so the
# band is open at the top and closer at the bottom: below `vref` on the
# approach is the one that kills people.
#
# **No band watches the flare, and that is deliberate.** The flare is where
# the speed is *meant* to go: the throttle closes, the nose comes up, and the
# aeroplane is flown onto the wheels as the speed decays. A first draft of
# this lesson held a speed band down to five feet and faulted every correct
# landing there is - the PA-28 crossed its threshold at 64 knots and touched
# at 37, which is a good landing, not a bad one. The speed is watched down to
# twenty feet and let alone after that.
#
# **The measurement, so the band can be checked rather than believed.** Down a
# three-degree glidepath from two miles, flown by the approach autopilot on
# each aeroplane own published reference speed, the four hold - against their
# own `vref` - between `vref-2` and `vref+5` on the approach, and between
# `vref` and `vref+5` over the threshold: the 172 (vref 60) flew 58-64 then
# 60-64, the 182 (vref 64) 61-69 then 64-69, the PA-28 (vref 64) 59-69 then
# 61-68. The band below is `vref-8` to `vref+12`, which is that with about six
# knots of margin at each end.
#
# **A first draft had `vref-5` at the bottom and the PA-28 failed it by a
# knot.** A band set one knot outside what a correct approach actually does is
# a band that faults good flying on a gusty day.

# **The approach watches the descent as well as the speed**, because every
# stage of this lesson ends on a height, and a crash reaches a height as surely
# as a landing does. An Airbus A380 once started clean at its landing speed,
# stalled, and fell from 688 feet to the runway in ten seconds at 6,800 ft/min
# - and was passed through all three stages, caught only because its speed
# band happened to break on the way down: it was falling at 113 feet a second.
# **Forty feet a second is set from what was measured**, not from the
# glidepath's arithmetic. A three-degree path at 200 knots asks for eighteen,
# but an aeroplane started on the approach is not trimmed for it, and in the
# first two or three seconds, before the elevator has caught it, the heavy
# ones sink harder - the A380 to 31 ft/s, the A320 to 27, the 737 to 25.
# Forty is room for that, and a long way short of a fall.
name Approach and landing in a light aeroplane
teaches light-aircraft

stage The approach
do Have the aerodrome in sight and join the approach on the runway heading
do Set the landing flap and check the undercarriage
do Hold the approach speed and let the aeroplane come down the glidepath
hold velocities/vc-kts vref-8 vref+12 Hold the approach speed
need velocities/h-dot-fps >= -30 Arrive over the threshold coming down the glidepath, not falling
until position/h-agl-ft <= 50

stage Over the threshold
do Cross the threshold at the approach speed, on the centreline
hold velocities/vc-kts vref-8 vref+12 Cross the threshold on speed
until position/h-agl-ft <= 20

stage The flare and the landing roll
do Close the throttle and raise the nose to check the descent
do Let the speed go: she should touch on the main wheels, nose high
do Hold her straight with the rudder, and brake once she is rolling
need fcs/throttle-cmd-norm <= 0.15 Close the throttle for the landing
until velocities/vc-kts <= 20
